# Updated plan: spec-first redesign of `targetuserspacecreator`

_Supersedes `project/battle-plan-for-babies-from-claude`. Incorporates the lessons in `project/refactor-retrospective.md` and the intent in `project/battle-plan-for-babies`._

## Context — why we are doing this again

The first attempt (commits `eb2eec6c`..`89ac43c5`, still checked out on branch
`post-armageddon-building-site-for-babies`, HEAD `89ac43c5`) **failed the user's intent**. The
brief asked for a *spec-first redesign* — write a specification of the actor's current behaviour,
then re-author clean code that still covers every use-case while dropping/replacing deprecated
content. What was delivered was a *behaviour-preserving file split*: the same spaghetti relocated,
largely verbatim, into six files (`1442 → 1488` logic LOC [Medium — figures from the retrospective]).

Root cause (retrospective §3/§7): **the failed plan made "the diff looks like a pure move" a
success criterion** (`git diff -M` must show pure renames). It measured similarity-to-old-code, so
it got similarity-to-old-code. The execution was faithful; the plan was wrong.

Intended outcome of this redo: the actor's external behaviour is unchanged (same consumed/produced
messages, same 5 inhibitors, same RHUI invariants), but the code is genuinely redesigned from a
written contract — god functions decomposed, the #486 duplicate-parse resolved, TODOs fixed not
relocated, deprecated content actually gone, shared leapp libraries reused. The `/etc/pki`
symlink-decoupling code is a frozen carve-out.

## The one rule that governs everything

> Write the behavioural contract first and measure the result against **behaviour**, never against
> similarity to the old code. "The diff looks like a move" is a **failure** signal, not a goal.

